  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Laya, a place which is located over 3,800 metres above sea level, is known for its thin air and harsh climate. A health assistant posted there observes that new staff often develop breathlessness, headache, and fatigue within the first few days of arrival. However, local residents (Layaps) seem unaffected and carry out physically demanding tasks with ease.
(a) What is the cause of high-altitude sickness? Describe two immediate physiological responses to this condition.
(b) Mention one chemical adaptation that helps the body adjust to high altitude over time, and explain how it improves oxygen delivery. (3 marks)', '(a) At high altitude the air pressure, and so the partial pressure of oxygen, is low, so less oxygen enters the blood (hypoxia), causing altitude sickness. Immediate responses: breathing becomes faster and deeper (hyperventilation) to take in more oxygen, and the heart rate and cardiac output rise to circulate blood faster. (b) Over days and weeks the kidneys release more erythropoietin, which increases red blood cells and haemoglobin, so each litre of blood carries more oxygen. Red cells also make more 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG), which helps haemoglobin release oxygen to the tissues.', null, '[{"point": "Cause: low atmospheric (partial) pressure of oxygen, so less O2 in blood (hypoxia)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Two immediate responses: faster/deeper breathing (hyperventilation) and increased heart rate / cardiac output", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Adaptation: more erythropoietin -> more RBCs and haemoglobin, or more 2,3-BPG, so blood carries/unloads more O2 to tissues", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
    324, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
