  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain the principle of blood pressure measurement using a sphygmomanometer. (3 marks)', 'A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure indirectly. An inflatable cuff around the upper arm is pumped above systolic pressure so the brachial artery is compressed and blood flow stops. A stethoscope is placed over the artery below the cuff and the pressure is slowly released. When cuff pressure falls just below systolic pressure, blood spurts through and turbulent flow produces tapping (Korotkoff) sounds: the reading at the first sound is the systolic pressure. As the pressure falls further, the sounds disappear when flow becomes smooth and continuous: this reading is the diastolic pressure. Values are read in mmHg (normal about 120/80).', null, '[{"point": "Cuff inflated around upper arm above systolic pressure, occluding the brachial artery (no flow, no sound)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Pressure slowly released; first Korotkoff sound (turbulent flow) = systolic pressure", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Sounds disappear when flow becomes smooth/continuous = diastolic pressure; read on mercury/aneroid gauge in mmHg (normal about 120/80)", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
